Z||000000||ChadMan||03-04-2001||09:38 AM||||I don't know what this EGR valve will fit, but it sure as hell won't fit a 1988 Pontiac Fiero with 2.5L 4-cylinder engine.  The spacing of the bolt holes and vent hole are totally different.<P>I ran through the EGR test listed in the Haynes manual and it looks like my old EGR valve is working.  I was really hoping a new EGR valve would cure my stumbling...no such luck.||||reg||8
Z||000001||theogre||03-04-2001||01:54 PM||||check MAT sensor in intake manifold to left of TBI. If it's even a little cooked or carboned replace it.<P>check MAP and it's plumbing carefully.<P>check PCV elbows. clamp all joints. don't over tighten the clamps or you'll wreck the elbows.||||reg||1
Z||000002||JOHN R. ALLEN III||03-05-2001||10:07 AM||||Chadman... Stay focused. Go to your local parts store... Get the right EGR.  It will solve the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||03-06-2001||07:48 AM||||I called the Fiero Store and they looked up the GM part number...somehow they mislabeled a few EGR valves.  They are going to pick up this one and send me the right one.<P>This is the third time they've screwed up on parts, but they're so helpful and friendly about making it right that I will keep on buying stuff there.  (Actually, as an 88 owner, I really don't have much of a choice!)<P>Hopefully next weekend I'll have the right valve and can try again.||||reg||1
